<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Kept in step with the release tag: release.yml names the published exe after the tag, and
      this is what puts the same number in the exe's file properties. Bump it in the same commit
      that gets tagged, or the download name and the binary disagree.
    -->
    <Version>4.3.0</Version>

    <!--
      Writes packages.lock.json next to each project, recording the exact resolved version and
      content hash of every direct and transitive dependency. CI restores in locked mode, so a
      dependency that silently changes underneath us fails the build instead of shipping.
      Regenerate intentionally by restoring with the "force-evaluate" switch.
    -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!--
      A local publish rewrites these files, and the rewritten shape breaks CI. That cannot be fixed
      from here, so it is written down instead.

      `dotnet publish -r win-x64` restores a RID-specific graph plus Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks and
      writes both into the lock files. The restore CI runs has no RID and sees neither, so locked
      mode against a publish-written lock file fails with NU1004: "the project's runtime identifiers
      have changed from. Project's runtime identifiers: , lock file's runtime identifiers win-x64".

      Turning this property off under a RID looks like the fix and is not. NuGet rejects
      RestorePackagesWithLockFile=false while a lock file exists, with NU1005, so every publish then
      fails outright. Tried, measured, reverted.

      What works is a habit: the committed lock files are the RID-less ones, so after publishing
      locally, put them back before committing.

          git restore **/packages.lock.json

      Committing a publish-written lock file is how this last broke, and it fails on the next push
      rather than locally, which is what makes it worth this much explanation.
    -->

    <!--
      Two target frameworks, named here rather than spelled out in each project, because they have
      to move together: the App references UI and Core, UI references Core, and the test project
      references Core, so a version raised in one place has to be raised everywhere or the
      references stop resolving.

      The 10.0.19041.0 part is what makes the WinRT projection available, which is needed for
      exactly one thing: Windows.Security.Credentials.KeyCredentialManager, the only API on Windows
      that can hold a secret behind a Hello gesture rather than merely asking for one. See
      HelloKeyProtector. The cost is a floor of Windows 10 2004 (May 2020), which the Windows build
      pays and the portable one does not.

      Which projects take which:

        RavensPort.UI    portable only — view models and the interfaces they reach the desktop
                         through, with no UI framework referenced at all
        RavensPort.Core  both — the Windows build adds Hello, Credential Manager and Authenticode
        RavensPort.App   both — the Windows build adds the WinForms tray icon
        Core.Tests       both, matching Core

      The portable target is what makes a Linux build possible; see .claude/LINUX-PORT-PLAN.md.
    -->
    <PortableTargetFramework>net8.0</PortableTargetFramework>
    <WindowsTargetFramework>net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0</WindowsTargetFramework>

    <!--
      Runs on anything from Windows 10 1809 up; only the Hello path needs 2004, and it checks.
      Conditional because it means nothing to the portable target, where declaring a Windows
      version floor would be a category error rather than a harmless extra.
    -->
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion Condition="$(TargetFramework.StartsWith('net8.0-windows'))">10.0.17763.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>

    <!--
      Lets a non-Windows machine *restore* these projects. It changes nothing on Windows, where the
      targeting packs come from the SDK; without it, a restore on Linux fails outright with
      NETSDK1100 rather than merely being unable to build.

      Needed because not every job that touches this repository is one we write. GitHub's Automatic
      Dependency Submission — the managed workflow that populates the dependency graph once
      Dependabot is enabled — runs `dotnet restore` on ubuntu against every csproj it finds, and it
      is not editable. Every project here targeted net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0 at the time, so it
      failed on all of them and submitted no graph at all, which quietly takes Dependabot alerts
      down with it.

      Still needed now that there is a portable target, because the Windows target is still in the
      list and a restore has to resolve both.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
